WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:21.650
<v Ira Glass>Someone who works with our radio show, Bianca Giaever, went to this presentation at Columbia University a while back where two people onstage puzzled over this thing they had found online called Wake Up Now. And I say "thing they found online," because the whole point of their presentation was it was hard to tell exactly what it was.
<v Ira Glass>一位与我们广播节目合作的同事，比安卡·贾维尔（Bianca Giaever），前阵子在哥伦比亚大学参加了一场演讲，台上两个人对着他们在网上发现的一个叫“唤醒现在”（Wake Up Now）的东西感到困惑。我之所以说“在网上发现的东西”，是因为他们演讲的核心重点就是，很难说清那到底是什么。

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:35.900
<v Woman>Why do some people live their lives in black and white while others seem to live in vibrant color? Well, part of it is knowing a secret, a secret that changes how people see the world. This video is your invitation to WakeUpNow.
<v Woman>为什么有些人过着黑白色的生活，而另一些人似乎生活在绚丽的色彩中？嗯，部分原因在于了解一个秘密，一个改变人们看待世界方式的秘密。这段视频就是你加入“唤醒现在”的邀请函。

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:55.220
<v Ira Glass>This is a video that was on WakeUpNow's website, which just looked like a kind of generic corporate-y website. And WakeUpNow seemed to be a company. But it was hard to tell exactly what the company did. This site talked about joining, about becoming a member, about living a better life. So was it a club? Was it an organization?
<v Ira Glass>这是“唤醒现在”网站上的一段视频，看起来就像是一个普通的企业网站。“唤醒现在”似乎是一家公司，但很难说清这家公司到底是做什么的。网站上谈论着加入、成为会员，以及过上更好的生活。所以，这是一家俱乐部吗？是一个组织吗？

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:11.720
<v Ira Glass>And then, when you googled WakeUpNow, there were tons of videos. Bianca saw some at the presentation at Columbia. And then, after the presentation, she started Googling on her own. And then, she sucked one of our producers, Brian Reed, into the vortex with her. Here is one of the videos they found.
<v Ira Glass>然后，当你用谷歌搜索“唤醒现在”时，会出现海量的视频。比安卡在哥伦比亚大学的演讲中看到了一些。演讲结束后，她开始自己上网搜索。接着，她把我们的制作人布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）也一起拉进了这个旋涡。这就是他们发现的视频之一。

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.770
<v Man 1>Hey, we're here in Cancun with the greatest team in the world, WakeUpNow. You should have been here. All the leaders are here. We're having fun. Beautiful island.
<v Man 1>嘿，我们现在在坎昆，和世界上最棒的团队“唤醒现在”在一起。你本该来的。所有的领导者都在这里。我们玩得很开心。美丽的岛屿。

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:35.690
<v Ira Glass>This is a video of a bunch of young people hanging out on the beach. You can see jet skis and the ocean. In the video, they refer to this as a leadership summit. And there was a whole genre of videos just like this, people in exotic locations, riding around in nice looking cars or, in this one, spelunking in a cave in Mexico, chanting.
<v Ira Glass>这是一段一群年轻人在海滩上闲逛的视频。你可以看到摩托艇和海洋。在视频中，他们称这为领导力峰会。像这样的视频类型有很多，人们出现在充满异国情调的地方，坐着看起来很高级的车，或者像这一段，在墨西哥的洞穴里探险，大声呼喊。

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:41.430
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "LA BAMBA"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “拉班巴”]

00:01:41.430 --> 00:02:09.800
<v Ira Glass>That is, of course, WakeUpNow with "La Bamba." And then, there were a lot of videos with the word "scam" in the title, like "WakeUpNow is a scam, for real exposed truth." And so, these two producers on our staff were like, oh, so this is some kind of internet scam. But then, when they watched the videos, it seemed very different. Like, take this one for example titled, "WakeUpNow Warning, Don't Join Until You See This."
<v Ira Glass>那当然是“唤醒现在”在唱《拉班巴》。此外，还有很多标题里带有“诈骗”字眼的视频，比如“‘唤醒现在’是骗局，揭露真实真相”。所以，我们制作组的这两位制作人当时心想，噢，原来这是某种互联网诈骗。但当他们观看视频时，情况似乎大不相同。比如，以这段视频为例，标题是“‘唤醒现在’警告，看这个之前不要加入”。

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.740
<v Man 2>WakeUpNow is one of the most legitimate companies out there. The reason why is because--
<v Man 2>“唤醒现在”是目前最合法的公司之一。原因在于——

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:36.360
<v Ira Glass>So "WakeUpNow Warning, Don't Join Till You See This" is actually not a warning at all but entirely about how great WakeUpNow is. And in fact, there were so many videos like this, a video called "Five Reasons Not to Join WakeUpNow" offered not a single reason why not to join WakeUpNow, but instead went on to describe what a super awesome company it is.
<v Ira Glass>所以这个“‘唤醒现在’警告，看这个之前不要加入”其实根本不是什么警告，而是完全在讲述“唤醒现在”有多棒。事实上，像这样的视频有很多，有一个视频叫“不要加入‘唤醒现在’的五个理由”，里面竟然没提供一个不加入的理由，反而继续描述这是一家多么超级棒的公司。

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.930
<v Ira Glass>So I'm joined in the studio right now by the two producers who were watching all these videos, Brian and Bianca. Hi, guys.
<v Ira Glass>现在和我一起在演播室里的，是观看了所有这些视频的两位制作人，布莱恩和比安卡。你们好。

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.440
<v Bianca Giaever>Hi.
<v Bianca Giaever>你好。

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:42.950
<v Brian Reed>Hey.
<v Brian Reed>嘿。

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.420
<v Ira Glass>So at that point, as best as you could tell, what was WakeUpNow?
<v Ira Glass>所以当时据你们所知，“唤醒现在”到底是什么？

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:56.000
<v Brian Reed>We honestly didn't know. Like, we couldn't figure it out. We eventually put some research in. We learned that, later, this move, the scam thing--
<v Brian Reed>我们真的不知道。我们研究不出来。我们后来做了一些调查。我们了解到，后来这种手段，就是诈骗那一套——

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.580
<v Ira Glass>You mean labeling videos "Such and Such is a Scam"?
<v Ira Glass>你是说把视频贴上“某某某是骗局”的标签？

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:14.450
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, exactly that. And then, having it not actually talk about it being a scam. So that's a move, I guess, that some people who are involved with certain companies or selling certain products will do. Like, that's a thing, I guess. But I'd never seen a video like that before. And it was really confusing.
<v Brian Reed>是的，正是那样。然后里面其实根本不提它是个骗局。我想，这大概是一些参与某些公司或销售某些产品的人会用的套路。这算是一种手段吧。但我以前从未见过这样的视频。这真的很让人困惑。

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:20.540
<v Bianca Giaever>I mean, in so many of these WakeUpNow videos, we could tell people we're pitching something. But as far as we could tell, it wasn't a product.
<v Bianca Giaever>我的意思是，在这么多“唤醒现在”的视频中，我们能看出人们在推销什么东西。但据我们所知，那并不是产品。

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:27.020
<v Panama>(SINGING) Build a bank, ah! I say, I say, I say, you need to build a bank, ah!
<v Panama>（歌唱）建立银行，啊！我说，我说，我说，你需要建立银行，啊！

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.060
<v Ira Glass>So what is this?
<v Ira Glass>所以这是什么？

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:36.680
<v Bianca Giaever>This is a video of a guy who calls himself Panama. And it's titled "WakeUpNow Scam, Lend a Hand, Build a Man, Woman."
<v Bianca Giaever>这是一个自称巴拿马（Panama）的家伙的视频。标题是“‘唤醒现在’骗局，伸出援手，成就男男女女”。

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:49.890
<v Panama>What are you doing? Eating carrots? Tomatoes? [SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Panama>你在干什么？吃胡萝卜？西红柿？[西班牙语]

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:59.030
<v Brian Reed>So yeah, it wasn't really clear what WakeUpNow was. But still, there were hundreds of these videos. It was like this whole subculture with all these people, about thousands of people.
<v Brian Reed>所以说，真的不清楚“唤醒现在”到底是什么。但即便如此，还是有数百个这样的视频。这就像是一个整个亚文化，有成千上万的人。

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.810
<v Ira Glass>OK, so then, I know what happened next is that you found out that WakeUpNow had a conference coming up, right?
<v Ira Glass>好的，接下来的事情我知道了，你们发现“唤醒现在”要举办一场会议，对吧？

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.890
<v Bianca Giaever>Yeah, and we went to try and figure out what this was.
<v Bianca Giaever>是的，我们去那里是想弄明白这到底是什么。

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:19.890
<v Brian Reed>Yeah, and what we eventually found out is that it's something called "network marketing." It's a network marketing company. But also what we found out is that that is a very bland term for something completely mind-blowing, way beyond anything we could have imagined.
<v Brian Reed>没错，我们最终发现这是一种叫做“网络营销”的东西。这是一家网络营销公司。但我们也发现，对于某种完全让人震惊、超乎我们想象的事物来说，这是一个非常平淡的术语。

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:34.370
<v Ira Glass>So today on our radio program, Wake Up Now. If nothing else, I have to say, that is a completely genius name for a company. It promises so much so efficiently. That you'll open your eyes. You're life will be totally different, totally better.
<v Ira Glass>所以，今天我们的广播节目谈论“唤醒现在”。如果不说别的，我必须说，这作为公司名字真是太天才了。它极其高效地做出了如此多的承诺。让你睁开眼睛。你的生活将会完全不同，完全更好。

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:57.830
<v Ira Glass>And so, today, as New Year's approaches, as lots of people take stock of who they've been and make resolutions about who they want to be, we have stories of people in very different contexts who try to wake up and wake up others. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>所以，今天随着新年临近，当许多人回顾过去并为想成为什么样的人做出决定时，我们有一些故事，讲述人们在完全不同的背景下试图醒来并唤醒他人。请继续收听。

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:12.020
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, Something's Happening Here and You Don't Know What It Is. So Brian and Bianca headed to WakeUpNow's conference in New York City at a hotel in Times Square to try to figure out what this was. Here they are, Brian and Bianca.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，这里发生了一些事，而你不知道是什么。所以布莱恩和比安卡前往位于纽约时代广场一家酒店举行的“唤醒现在”会议，试图弄清楚这到底是什么。他们就在现场，布莱恩和比安卡。

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:22.100
<v Brian Reed>I'm a little embarrassed to admit this. But even after being at this conference for several hours, if you put a gun to my head and asked me what WakeUpNow is, what it does, I still don't think I could have told you.
<v Brian Reed>我很尴尬地承认这一点。但即使在这个会议待了几个小时后，如果有人拿枪指着我的头问我“唤醒现在”是什么、它是做什么的，我仍然觉得我无法告诉你。

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:29.450
<v Bianca Giaever>We talked to dozens of participants. And what we learned is that people were incredibly excited about something they weren't terribly good at explaining.
<v Bianca Giaever>我们采访了数十名参与者。我们了解到的是，人们对他们无法准确解释的事情感到难以置信的兴奋。

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.770
<v Woman 1>Why do WakeUpNow? This is a new beginning for the new generation.
<v Woman 1>为什么要加入“唤醒现在”？这是新一代的全新开始。

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.910
<v Bianca Giaever>What is it? I don't even know what it is.
<v Bianca Giaever>它是什么？我甚至不知道它是什么。

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.700
<v Woman 1>Oh, it's a software company.
<v Woman 1>噢，它是一家软件公司。

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.760
<v Bianca Giaever>It's a software company, you said?
<v Bianca Giaever>你说它是一家软件公司？

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.490
<v Woman 1>I think.
<v Woman 1>我想是吧。

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:57.090
<v Bianca Giaever>People had come from all over, East Coast, Minnesota, California. They're mostly young and mostly people of color. They called WakeUpNow a lifestyle. They talked about WUN life, W-U-N, for WakeUpNow.
<v Bianca Giaever>人们从各地赶来，东海岸、明尼苏达州、加利福尼亚州。他们大多很年轻，大多是有色人种。他们把“唤醒现在”称为一种生活方式。他们谈论“WUN生活”，W-U-N，代表“唤醒现在”。

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:09.580
<v Brian Reed>For the longest time, I couldn't even figure out what to call the hundreds of people at this conference. Were they customers of WakeUpNow? Employees? Card-carrying members? Whatever they were, some of them could list specific WakeUpNow products.
<v Brian Reed>很长一段时间里，我甚至不知道该如何称呼这次会议上的数百人。他们是“唤醒现在”的客户吗？员工？持证会员？无论他们是什么，其中一些人能列出具体的“唤醒现在”产品。

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.390
<v Brian Reed>And the product is?
<v Brian Reed>产品是什么？

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:12.990
<v Man 1>Awaken Thunder.
<v Man 1>Awaken Thunder（觉醒雷霆）。

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.780
<v Brian Reed>What's Wake and Thunder?
<v Brian Reed>什么是Awaken Thunder？

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:14.690
<v Man 1>Awaken Thunder. It's--
<v Man 1>Awaken Thunder。它是——

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:15.270
<v Brian Reed>What is that?
<v Brian Reed>那是什么？

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.410
<v Man 1>--an energy drink. An energy drink.
<v Man 1>——一种能量饮料。一种能量饮料。

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.040
<v Brian Reed>Oh, so you're selling an energy drink.
<v Brian Reed>噢，所以你们在卖能量饮料。

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.670
<v Man 1>We're not selling it. You can buy it.
<v Man 1>我们不是在卖它。你可以买它。

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:27.150
<v Brian Reed>OK. I'm just try-- so are you guys going to work for the company? Or you're buying the product?
<v Brian Reed>好的。我只是想——所以你们是要去这家公司工作吗？还是你们在买产品？

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:35.400
<v Man 1>Well, we don't have to buy the product. So what you do is you just go around and spread the word about it.
<v Man 1>嗯，我们不必买产品。所以你要做的就是四处宣传它。

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.280
<v Brian Reed>But are you selling an energy drink, though? I'm confused.
<v Brian Reed>但你们是在卖能量饮料吗？我困惑了。

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.270
<v Man 1>No, we don't have to sell it.
<v Man 1>不，我们不必卖它。

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.530
<v Man 2>The company offers it.
<v Man 2>公司提供它。

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:43.070
<v Man 1>Yeah.
<v Man 1>是的。

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:48.280
<v Man 2>The company offers it for you. It's exclusive only for the people that are in WakeUpNow.
<v Man 2>公司为你提供。它只对那些加入“唤醒现在”的人独家供应。

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:55.830
<v Bianca Giaever>So there are products. And these people encouraged other people to buy them. But when we tried to use the word "selling," we were corrected.
<v Bianca Giaever>所以有产品。这些人鼓励其他人购买它们。但当我们试图使用“销售”这个词时，我们被纠正了。

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:03.470
<v Woman 2>Yeah, I mean, I don't like saying "selling." I like using "providing." I provide people with it.
<v Woman 2>是的，我的意思是，我不喜欢说“销售”。我喜欢用“提供”。我向人们提供它。

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.420
<v Bianca Giaever>Do they pay you money in exchange?
<v Bianca Giaever>他们会付钱给你作为交换吗？

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.590
<v Woman 2>No, not really. I got a commission from it. That's  about it.
<v Woman 2>不，并不是真的。我从中得到佣金。就这些。

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:19.710
<v Brian Reed>Awaken Thunder energy drink was not the only thing WakeUpNow was not selling, but providing. People seemed to be listing a totally random assortment of products.
<v Brian Reed>Awaken Thunder能量饮料并不是“唤醒现在”唯一没有在销售、而是在“提供”的东西。人们似乎在列出一堆完全随机的产品。

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:28.040
<v Bianca Giaever>Lots of things people said WakeUpNow offered were just discounts, off their AT&T phone bills, purchases at Office Depot, on groceries.
<v Bianca Giaever>人们说“唤醒现在”提供的许多东西只是折扣，AT&T电话费折扣、Office Depot购物折扣、杂货折扣。

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.510
<v Man 3>We have the Trivanis.
<v Man 3>我们有Trivanis。

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:30.080
<v Brian Reed>What are those?
<v Brian Reed>那些是什么？

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.500
<v Man 3>They're like lotion, skin products.
<v Man 3>它们就像乳液、护肤品。

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.250
<v Woman 3>And then, we have the coffee.
<v Woman 3>然后，我们有咖啡。

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.230
<v Man 4>We have cyber-protection.
<v Man 4>我们有网络安全保护。

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:41.780
<v Man 5>Have you ever wanted to learn another language? well we have a program that has 80 languages, similar to Rosetta Stone.
<v Man 5>你曾经想学习另一种语言吗？好吧，我们有一个拥有80种语言的程序，类似于罗塞塔石碑（Rosetta Stone）。

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:43.040
<v Bianca Giaever>There's a vacation club.
<v Bianca Giaever>有一个度假俱乐部。

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.350
<v Man 5>For example, I'm going to Florida. Hotel's usually $1,100. I'm going to pay $290 for the whole week, a condo.
<v Man 5>例如，我要去佛罗里达。酒店通常要1100美元。我整个星期只要支付290美元，住公寓。

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.190
<v Brian Reed>There's tax software.
<v Brian Reed>有税务软件。

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:57.950
<v Man 6>Most people can increase their tax deduction by $3,000 to $6,000 per year, just by the tax part alone.
<v Man 6>大多数人每年可以增加3000到6000美元的税收抵扣，仅通过税务部分就能实现。

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:06.950
<v Bianca Giaever>But in this sea of hard-to-understand information and unrelated products, one thing everyone seemed to agree on, WakeUpNow had a mission.
<v Bianca Giaever>但在这一片难以理解的信息和不相关的产品海洋中，有一点似乎每个人都同意：这就是“唤醒现在”有一个使命。

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:13.400
<v Man 7>WakeUpNow actually is a financial wellness company, showing people how to save, manage, and make money.
<v Man 7>“唤醒现在”实际上是一家财务健康公司，向人们展示如何储蓄、管理和赚钱。

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.890
<v Brian Reed>In fact, so many people recited this mission so precisely, it was actually a bit weird.
<v Brian Reed>事实上，有那么多人如此精确地背诵这个使命，这确实有点怪异。

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:19.640
<v Man 8>We'll save money, manage money, and make money.
<v Man 8>我们将省钱、管理金钱和赚钱。

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.620
<v Man 9>Save money, make money, and manage your money.
<v Man 9>省钱、赚钱和管理你的钱。

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.880
<v Man 10>Save, manage, and make money.
<v Man 10>省钱、管理和赚钱。

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.250
<v Man 11>Save money, manage money, and make more money.
<v Man 11>省钱、管理金钱，并赚更多的钱。

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:30.260
<v Man 12>Save, manage, and make money at the same time.
<v Man 12>同时省钱、管理和赚钱。

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:51.830
<v Brian Reed>It took us all day and, honestly, quite a bit more research afterwards, including signing up ourselves-- which was really complicated-- to piece together how WakeUpNow works. Like I said earlier, they're a network marketing company. And if you haven't heard that term, you've probably heard of Amway or Herbalife or New-Skin, companies where you sell things to people you know. That's network marketing.
<v Brian Reed>我们花了一整天，老实说，之后还做了不少研究，包括我们自己注册——这真的非常复杂——才拼凑出“唤醒现在”是如何运作的。就像我之前说的，他们是一家网络营销公司。如果你没听说过这个术语，你可能听说过安利（Amway）、康宝莱（Herbalife）或如新（New-Skin），这些公司就是向你认识的人兜售东西。这就是网络营销。

00:08:51.830 --> 00:09:10.770
<v Brian Reed>When you join WakeUpNow, you get access to a place on their website called The Hub, which is where you can purchase many of the products and services we heard about. You can join for free. But you're encouraged to sign up on a plan where you pay between $80 and $150 a month. You pay more money, you get more stuff, like the financial management and tax software.
<v Brian Reed>当你加入“唤醒现在”，你就可以进入他们网站上一个叫“枢纽”（The Hub）的地方，在那里你可以购买我们听到的许多产品和服务。你可以免费加入。但我们鼓励你注册一个每月支付80到150美元的计划。你付的钱越多，得到的东西就越多，比如财务管理和税务软件。

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:27.560
<v Bianca Giaever>So that's the saving money and managing money part of WakeUpNow. As for the making money part, you can make money by recruiting other people to join WakeUpNow. They buy stuff, you can earn commission. They recruit people, you make money off that too. A chain forms below you.
<v Bianca Giaever>所以这就是“唤醒现在”的省钱和管理金钱部分。至于赚钱部分，你可以通过招募其他人加入“唤醒现在”来赚钱。他们购买东西，你可以赚取佣金。他们招募其他人，你也能从中赚钱。你下方形成了一条链条。

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:46.190
<v Bianca Giaever>That's the network in network marketing. You get three people to join, you get $100. If each of them gets three people to join, that's when the real money kicks in, about $600 a month. There are other ways to earn that money. But that's the one we saw them push.
<v Bianca Giaever>这就是网络营销中的“网络”。你让三个人加入，你得到100美元。如果他们每个人都让三个人加入，那就是真正的钱开始涌入的时候，大约每月600美元。还有其他赚钱方式。但这是我们看到他们推崇的一种。

00:09:46.190 --> 00:10:15.270
<v Brian Reed>I know, this may sound like a pyramid scheme. Whether it is one, though, would have to be decided by the courts. We called the Federal Trade Commission. And a spokesman said figuring out if a network marketing company is a pyramid scheme is very difficult and takes a long investigation. The FTC's website says a good tip off that a network marketing company might be a pyramid scheme is that members earn more money for recruiting new members than for selling products to the public.
<v Brian Reed>我知道，这听起来可能像传销。不过它是否是传销，必须由法院决定。我们给联邦贸易委员会（FTC）打了电话。一位发言人表示，判断一家网络营销公司是否为传销非常困难，需要长时间的调查。FTC的网站说，一家网络营销公司可能是传销的一个很好的迹象是，成员因招募新成员赚的钱比向公众销售产品赚的钱更多。

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:29.860
<v Brian Reed>WakeUpNow's chief marketing officer told us that 100% of the company's profits come from product sales. But when you pay a monthly fee to join, they consider that a product. He said WakeUpNow is absolutely not a pyramid scheme.
<v Brian Reed>“唤醒现在”的首席营销官告诉我们，公司100%的利润来自产品销售。但当你支付月费加入时，他们将其视为产品。他说“唤醒现在”绝对不是传销。

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:38.080
<v Bianca Giaever>For now though, WakeUpNow members are charging forth, recruiting more and more people into the company and, apparently, getting paid a boatload to do it.
<v Bianca Giaever>不过目前，“唤醒现在”的成员正在奋力前进，招募越来越多的人进入公司，并且显然，为此赚取了巨额财富。

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.590
<v Bianca Giaever>Boom, boom, boom. Just watch, probably 500K a month walked by you.
<v Bianca Giaever>轰，轰，轰。看好了，走过你身边的可能就是月入50万的人。

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:48.220
<v Brian Reed>This guy's pointing at a group of otherwise ordinary looking people. He's talking about all their salaries added together.
<v Brian Reed>这个家伙指着一群看起来很普通的人。他说的是他们所有人的薪水总和。

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:49.860
<v Brian Reed>500K a month?
<v Brian Reed>月入50万？

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:53.550
<v Man 13>500K a month just walked by you, under 25-years-old.
<v Man 13>月入50万刚从你身边走过，25岁以下。

00:10:53.550 --> 00:11:07.000
<v Man 14>This company is making history actually. This company is creating the largest amount of under 30-year-old 6-figure-earner that I've ever seen. Look, 6-figure-earner, 6-figure-earner.
<v Man 14>这家公司实际上正在创造历史。这家公司正在创造我所见过的最多数量的30岁以下6位数收入者。看，6位数收入者，6位数收入者。

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:23.430
<v Bianca Giaever>People loved pointing out all the 6-figure-earner magnates among us. In our first few minutes of the conference, I saw this big bearded man. Someone else called him, quote, "a freaking duck hunter guy," which seemed like an apt description. People lined up to take pictures with him. Someone nearby told me why.
<v Bianca Giaever>人们喜欢指出我们中间所有的6位数收入大亨。在会议的前几分钟，我看到了这个大胡子男人。有人称他为“那个该死的养鸭人”，这似乎是一个恰当的描述。人们排队和他合影。附近有人告诉我原因。

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.910
<v Man 15>My boy right here is making $114,000 a month right now, $114,000.
<v Man 15>我这位兄弟现在月收入11.4万美元，11.4万。

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.650
<v Bianca Giaever>$114,000?
<v Bianca Giaever>11.4万？

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:32.790
<v Man 15>$114,000 a month.
<v Man 15>一个月11.4万。

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:53.900
<v Bianca Giaever>His name? See More Green. That's S-E-E M-O-R-E Green. See More is the top earner in WakeUpNow. He's been doing it a year. He's 25. Everybody knew who he was and how much he allegedly made. Later in the day, WakeUpNow gave See More Green an award for being number one.
<v Bianca Giaever>他的名字？See More Green（意为：赚更多钱）。拼写是S-E-E M-O-R-E Green。See More是“唤醒现在”收入最高的人。他做了一年了。他25岁。每个人都知道他是谁，以及他据称赚了多少钱。当天晚些时候，“唤醒现在”给See More Green颁发了一个奖项，因为他是第一名。

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:54.900
<v Man 16>(CHANTING) See More!
<v Man 16>（呼喊）See More！

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:55.400
<v Man 17>See More Green!
<v Man 17>See More Green！

00:11:55.400 --> 00:12:01.380
<v Man 16>See More! See More! See More! See More! See More! See More!
<v Man 16>See More！See More！See More！See More！See More！See More！

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.810
<v Man 16>[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Man 16>[欢呼和掌声]

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.730
<v Woman 4>Wow!
<v Woman 4>哇！

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:10.870
<v Bianca Giaever>A woman near me says, he's a regular dude. He's wearing a t-shirt. You would never know.
<v Bianca Giaever>我身边的一位女士说，他是个普通人。他穿着T恤。你永远看不出来。

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:11.430
<v Woman 5>You would never know.
<v Woman 5>你永远看不出来。

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.580
<v Woman 6>You'd never know, right?
<v Woman 6>你永远看不出来，对吧？

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.720
<v Woman 6>[CHEERING]
<v Woman 6>[欢呼]

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:27.140
<v Brian Reed>Another thing we encountered at the conference was a repulsion to something so unsavory, so unseemly, that WakeUpNow's emcee for the day couldn't even bear to speak the word aloud. So he spelled it out instead.
<v Brian Reed>我们在会议上遇到的另一件事是对某种令人不快、如此不得体的事情的排斥，以至于“唤醒现在”当天的司仪甚至无法忍受大声说出这个词。所以他把它拼了出来。

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:33.710
<v Man 17>There are so many people in a J-O-B that are bored. There's no reason they just another one of them.
<v Man 17>有太多人在做一份J-O-B（工作），感到厌烦。没有理由他们只是其中之一。

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:39.170
<v Brian Reed>J-O-B. Quick warning, I'm going to say the word right now, job.
<v Brian Reed>J-O-B。小提醒，我现在要说这个词了，工作（job）。

00:12:39.170 --> 00:13:01.110
<v Man 17>When you go into a store and you run into an employee with a J-O-B, and you can see it in their face. There is no energy, because they're not invested in what they're doing. It's not their business. I'm looking at a room full of people who care, because it's their business. It is their future. That is why I love this industry.
<v Man 17>当你走进一家商店遇到一个有J-O-B的员工，你可以从他们的脸上看出来。没有任何活力，因为他们没有投入到他们正在做的事情中。这不是他们的生意。我看到的是一屋子关心的人，因为这是他们的生意。这是他们的未来。这就是我热爱这个行业的原因。

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:08.330
<v Bianca Giaever>This dissatisfaction with 9-to-5s ran deep through the crowd. Take this paralegal. How does she describe her job?
<v Bianca Giaever>这种对“朝九晚五”的不满在人群中根深蒂固。看看这个法律助理。她怎么描述她的工作？

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:18.000
<v Woman 7>It's a soul-sucking, like, life-destroying, "sit at a desk all day and get fat" waste of life.
<v Woman 7>那是一份灵魂被吞噬、像是毁灭生活、“整天坐在桌子旁发胖”的人生浪费。

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.250
<v Bianca Giaever>She told me that as soon as her income from WakeUpNow inches above her current salary, she's outta there.
<v Bianca Giaever>她告诉我，只要她从“唤醒现在”获得的收入稍微超过她目前的工资，她就会离开那里。

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:32.250
<v Woman 7>It could be, like, $1 more than I'm making now. I'm quitting. Like, literally, $1. OK, I quit. I'm not coming in ever.
<v Woman 7>哪怕只比我现在赚的多1美元。我就辞职。真的，1美元。好吧，我不干了。我再也不来了。

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:47.800
<v Brian Reed>Of course, lots of jobs do suck. A surprising number of attendees at the conference told us they were planning on quitting to pursue WakeUpNow. These appear to be people with steady full-time E-M-P-L-O-Y-M-E-N-T, a compliance manager at a big bank, a human resources person at Coca-Cola.
<v Brian Reed>当然，很多工作确实很烂。在会议上，令人惊讶数量的参加者告诉我们，他们正计划辞职去追求“唤醒现在”。这些人似乎都有稳定的全职E-M-P-L-O-Y-M-E-N-T（就业），一家大银行的合规经理，可口可乐的人力资源人员。

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:55.980
<v Bianca Giaever>And we also met people who had actually done it. One person quit a restaurant. Another quit his sales job at Samsung. Plus, there was this guy.
<v Bianca Giaever>我们也遇到了真正做到这一点的人。一个人辞掉了餐馆的工作。另一个人辞掉了他在三星的销售工作。此外，还有这个人。

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.030
<v Antonio Johnson>Antonio "Unemployable" Johnson.
<v Antonio Johnson>安东尼奥“无法雇佣的”约翰逊（Antonio "Unemployable" Johnson）。

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:02.460
<v Bianca Giaever>He quit his job two years ago. Now, he has a motto.
<v Bianca Giaever>他两年前辞职了。现在，他有一句座右铭。

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:07.600
<v Antonio Johnson>Friends don't let friends get jobs.
<v Antonio Johnson>朋友不让朋友去找工作。

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:24.990
<v Brian Reed>It's clear why lots of people would want to leave their current lousy jobs. What's not so clear is why they'd choose to leave them for WakeUpNow. When you look at the WakeUpNow compensation plan, which we did later, it actually says in there how many members are making a small profit, 4%. That's it.
<v Brian Reed>很明显，为什么很多人想离开他们目前糟糕的工作。不太清楚的是，为什么他们会选择为“唤醒现在”离开。当你查看“唤醒现在”的薪酬计划时，我们后来做了，里面实际上说了有多少成员在赚取小额利润，4%。就这些。

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:43.600
<v Brian Reed>And those 4% are not making a living, just an average of about $600 a month. The number of people making anything close to resembling a living is about 1%. Those are the company's numbers. If they're accurate, presumably 95% of the people chanting in this conference room weren't making any profit whatsoever.
<v Brian Reed>而且这4%的人并没有以此为生，平均每月只有600美元左右。接近以此为生的人数大约是1%。这是公司的数据。如果它们准确的话，大概会议室里呼喊的95%的人根本没有赚到任何利润。

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:53.140
<v Crowd>(CHANTING) WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow! WakeUpNow!
<v Crowd>（呼喊）唤醒现在！唤醒现在！唤醒现在！唤醒现在！唤醒现在！唤醒现在！唤醒现在！

00:14:53.140 --> 00:15:04.790
<v Bianca Giaever>This is the beginning of the main event, what was billed as a business presentation that stretched on for nearly five hours with more than a dozen speakers. A lot of it was about how to recruit people into WakeUpNow.
<v Bianca Giaever>这是主活动的开始，被称为商业演示，持续了近五个小时，有十几位演讲者。其中很多内容是关于如何招募人们进入“唤醒现在”。

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:17.900
<v Bianca Giaever>I should say, officially, the company doesn't use the word "recruit." But there's no other way to describe what we saw. It was about recruiting. Like this advice from a guy named Rob McFadden. He said that, on the day you launch your WakeUpNow business--
<v Bianca Giaever>我应该正式说明，公司不使用“招募”这个词。但没有其他词可以描述我们所看到的。这确实是在招募。比如这个叫罗布·麦克法登（Rob McFadden）的人的建议。他说，在你启动“唤醒现在”业务的那天——

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:32.480
<v Rob Mcfadden>You set up a conference call at 10:00 AM. You invite everybody you know to be on that call. On that call, all you tell them is, I'm launching. We're going nuts. I can't even tell you what we're doing. I have a call scheduled at noon. Who's going to be on it?
<v Rob Mcfadden>你安排一个上午10点的电话会议。你邀请你认识的所有人参加那个电话。在那个电话里，你只需要告诉他们，我要启动了。我们要疯狂起来了。我甚至不能告诉你我们在做什么。我定了一个中午的电话。谁会参加？

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:45.080
<v Brian Reed>The next step was to get each person on the 10:00 AM call to bring 10 more people with them to the noon call and, then, to do the exact same thing over again for a 2 o'clock call, all with no mention of what the business actually is. After all--
<v Brian Reed>下一步是让上午10点电话会议上的每个人再带10个人参加中午的电话会议，然后，为下午2点的电话会议做完全相同的事情，全程不提这个生意到底是什么。毕竟——

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:50.240
<v Rob Mcfadden>People care more about how you say something that anything you ever say.
<v Rob Mcfadden>人们关心你说话的方式，胜过你所说的任何话。

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:59.750
<v Bianca Giaever>The fact that they called this event a business presentation was strange, because there was so little actual business discussed. So much of it was just motivational speeches.
<v Bianca Giaever>他们称这次活动为商业演示的事实很奇怪，因为讨论的实际业务很少。其中大部分只是励志演讲。

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:08.930
<v Man 18>And you being here today, I got to give you props. Because you get it. You get it. You're changing lives. You're starting a movement.
<v Man 18>你今天能在这里，我得给你点赞。因为你懂。你懂。你正在改变生活。你正在发起一场运动。

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:13.730
<v Rob Mcfadden>You don't got a dream, you don't have a roadmap. You don't have a roadmap, you ain't taking a trip.
<v Rob Mcfadden>你没有梦想，就没有路线图。你没有路线图，你就没在旅行。

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:20.450
<v Man 19>See, what is it that drives you? Is it feeding starving kids? Is it making a difference in this world?
<v Man 19>看，是什么驱动着你？是喂饱饥饿的孩子吗？是在这个世界上有所作为吗？

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:30.830
<v Man 20>I saw a vision to help people. I saw a vision to change the world. You have the ability to speak life or death into people. WakeUpNow is  speaking life.
<v Man 20>我看到了帮助人们的愿景。我看到了改变世界的愿景。你有能力向人们传达生命或死亡。“唤醒现在”正在传达生命。

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:48.390
<v Bianca Giaever>This was the aspect of the conference that we really struggled to make sense of, the contrast between this vision of WakeUpNow changing the world, speaking life into people, and the reality of what it seemed like WakeUpNow is actually doing, which is selling energy drinks and giving people discounts on random stuff.
<v Bianca Giaever>这是会议中我们真正努力去理解的方面，即“唤醒现在”改变世界、向人们传达生命的愿景，与“唤醒现在”实际似乎在做的事情（即销售能量饮料并给人们提供随机东西的折扣）之间的反差。

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:49.870
<v Jordan Harris>Now, product announcements, guys. It wouldn't be--
<v Jordan Harris>现在，产品发布，各位。如果不是——

00:16:49.870 --> 00:17:01.070
<v Bianca Giaever>This crystallized during the single most confusing moment of the conference, the big announcement they made in between inspirational speeches. The company's marketing director, Jordan Harris, took the stage.
<v Bianca Giaever>这在会议中最令人困惑的时刻结晶了，他们在励志演讲之间做出的重大宣布。公司的营销总监乔丹·哈里斯（Jordan Harris）走上舞台。

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:06.060
<v Jordan Harris>Nobody else outside this room knows anything about what we're about to talk about.
<v Jordan Harris>除了这个房间里的人，没有人知道我们要谈论什么。

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:14.180
<v Brian Reed>And what he went on to talk about was coupons. WakeUpNow members have access to coupons that they can print out and use to get money off at local businesses.
<v Brian Reed>他继续谈论的是优惠券。 “唤醒现在”成员可以访问优惠券，他们可以打印出来，用于在当地企业消费时抵扣钱。

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:22.250
<v Jordan Harris>How many of you guys have smartphones? OK, pull out your smartphones real quick. How cool would it be if, instead of having to print the  coupon, you just showed your smartphone.
<v Jordan Harris>你们当中有多少人有智能手机？好的，快速拿出你们的智能手机。如果不需要打印优惠券，而是直接展示你的智能手机，那该多酷啊。

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.170
<v Jordan Harris>[CHEERING]
<v Jordan Harris>[欢呼]

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:34.970
<v Jordan Harris>Right? This is awesome. So guys, we're excited. Today, we're announcing our new iPhone and Android app that's going to let you do just that.
<v Jordan Harris>对吧？这太棒了。所以各位，我们很兴奋。今天，我们要宣布我们新的iPhone和安卓应用，它将让你做到这一点。

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:40.650
<v Jordan Harris>[LOUD CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Jordan Harris>[大声欢呼和掌声]

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:45.630
<v Brian Reed>The big announcement was that you no longer had to print the coupons out. You could now just use a smartphone app.
<v Brian Reed>重大宣布是，你不再需要打印优惠券。你现在只需使用智能手机应用程序。

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:51.120
<v Jordan Harris>Let's talk about when it's going to launch. OK, it's going to be available-- two weeks.
<v Jordan Harris>让我们谈谈它什么时候推出。好的，它将在两周内可用。

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:55.330
<v Jordan Harris>[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]
<v Jordan Harris>[欢呼和掌声]

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:59.650
<v Jordan Harris>What do you think? I think we can do better. Let's just do it today.
<v Jordan Harris>你们觉得呢？我觉得我们可以做得更好。今天就做吧。

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:04.440
<v Jordan Harris>[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]
<v Jordan Harris>[欢呼和掌声]

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:11.630
<v Jordan Harris>That's right. You can go in the iPhone store and Android store and download this today!
<v Jordan Harris>没错。你们今天就可以去iPhone商店和安卓商店下载这个！

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:19.730
<v Bianca Giaever>After this, Brian and I had to check with each other. We honestly weren't sure if we had missed something. This excitement was over coupons?
<v Bianca Giaever>在此之后，布莱恩和我不得不互相核对。我们真的不确定是否错过了什么。这种兴奋是因为优惠券？

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:24.780
<v Brian Reed>The emcee couldn't even move on with the presentation after this, because everyone was so overwhelmed.
<v Brian Reed>司仪在此之后甚至无法继续演讲，因为每个人都太不知所措了。

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:43.130
<v Emcee>Wow, that is-- I'm-- I'm beside myself. I know, you're all looking. It's OK, take your time. Wow. All right, we're all catching our breath. It's OK. Yeah, day might as well be over for some of you.
<v Emcee>哇，那是——我——我难以自持。我知道，你们都在看。没关系，慢慢来。哇。好了，我们都在喘口气。没关系。是的，对你们中的一些人来说，这一天大概也就这样了。

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:55.970
<v Bianca Giaever>We left the conference not sure what to think of what we'd witnessed. But the fact that people were talking about quitting their jobs to do WakeUpNow, when the chances of making any kind of living were so low, that seemed alarming.
<v Bianca Giaever>离开会议时，我们不知道该如何看待我们所目睹的一切。但人们谈论辞掉工作去做“唤醒现在”，而过上任何体面生活的机会却如此渺茫，这看起来令人担忧。

00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:06.950
<v Brian Reed>And to our surprise, when we interviewed WakeUpNow's Chief Marketing Officer Jordan Harris, the guy who made the coupon announcement, he said that isn't meant to be the company's message. They don't want everyone quitting their jobs.
<v Brian Reed>令我们惊讶的是，当我们采访“唤醒现在”的首席营销官乔丹·哈里斯（即宣布优惠券的那个人）时，他说这并不是公司的初衷。他们不希望每个人都辞掉工作。

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:24.590
<v Jordan Harris>There's an element of any live event, like we have, where sometimes people get up there and they get excited. And there's things that you'd rather have them not say. Our standard advice for people, you should be replacing your income for at least three to four months consistently through WakeUpNow before you consider it.
<v Jordan Harris>任何现场活动都有一个因素，像我们有的一样，有时人们会站在上面，他们会很兴奋。有些事情你宁愿他们别说。我们给人们的标准建议是，在考虑辞职之前，你应该通过“唤醒现在”持续至少三到四个月来替代你的收入。

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:35.060
<v Brian Reed>So on WakeUpNow's Income Disclosure Statement, it says that 4% of members make a profit. Do you think most people who are doing WakeUpNow as a business know that number?
<v Brian Reed>那么在“唤醒现在”的收入披露声明上，写着4%的成员获得了利润。你认为大多数做“唤醒现在”作为生意的人知道那个数字吗？

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:41.180
<v Jordan Harris>I think, no question. That is so widely disseminated. You can go pull income disclosure statements from--
<v Jordan Harris>我想，毫无疑问。那被广泛传播。你可以去获取收入披露声明——

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:45.530
<v Brian Reed>You guys-- like, I don't think you mentioned it aloud at the conference.
<v Brian Reed>你们——就像，我不认为你们在会议上大声提到过。

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:48.380
<v Jordan Harris>Yeah, we don't mention it aloud. But it's like, it is--
<v Jordan Harris>是的，我们没有大声提到它。但就像，它是——

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:48.530
<v Brian Reed>Why not?
<v Brian Reed>为什么不呢？

00:19:48.530 --> 00:20:10.550
<v Jordan Harris>--on our website, on every-- eh? In a conference, when you're out there talking to them, it didn't come up. We're at a conference. We're trying to have high energy. We're there to have a lot of fun. That's not something that is pitched from stage. But we make sure that everybody is aware of it. I don't know if you saw to stage left. There was an income disclosure statement on the wall. It was in the booklet that everybody got.
<v Jordan Harris>——在我们的网站上，在每一处——呃？在会议中，当你在外面和他们交谈时，它没提起来。我们在会议中。我们试图保持高能量。我们在那里是为了玩得很开心。那不是台上推销的东西。但我们确保每个人都知道它。我不知道你是否看到舞台左侧。墙上有一份收入披露声明。在每个人拿到的小册子里也有。

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:26.120
<v Jordan Harris>So everybody left with that income disclosure statement in their hand. We don't want people joining with the idea that, hey, you promised me, if I joined, I would make this amount. That's not what we care about. We care about changing your financial situation from the products. And if you'd like to supplement it by making money, you have an opportunity.
<v Jordan Harris>所以每个人离开时手里都拿着那份收入披露声明。我们不希望人们带着这种想法加入，嘿，你向我承诺，如果我加入，我会赚这么多钱。那不是我们在意的。我们在意的是通过产品改变你的财务状况。如果你想通过赚钱来补充它，你有一个机会。

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:46.160
<v Bianca Giaever>We told Jordan this wasn't the measured message we left the conference with. Instead, in a room where less than 1% was making any real money from WakeUpNow, we heard speakers spinning a fantasy of enormous wealth, infinity pools, trips to Cancun. One speaker had the crowd shout out their favorite fancy cars, Maseratis and Porsches.
<v Bianca Giaever>我们告诉乔丹，这不是我们离开会议时得到的衡量过的信息。相反，在一个不到1%的人从“唤醒现在”赚到任何真正金钱的房间里，我们听到演讲者在编造财富的幻想，无边泳池，去坎昆的旅行。一位演讲者让群众大声喊出他们最喜欢的豪车，玛莎拉蒂和保时捷。

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:59.960
<v Bianca Giaever>Jordan understood why we got this impression. But he says that's not WakeUpNow is really about. He says their company was built around the idea of helping people make an extra $500 or $600 a month and that they chose that number deliberately.
<v Bianca Giaever>乔丹明白为什么我们会有这种印象。但他说是那不是“唤醒现在”真正关于的内容。他说他们的公司建立在帮助人们每月额外赚取500或600美元的想法上，他们是故意选择这个数字的。

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:21.470
<v Jordan Harris>Because we look at the statistics and the majority of bankruptcies could avoid it if they had $500 more per month. And so, really, if we can get a ton of people that are earning $500 to $600 a month, then for us, that's our sweet spot. We're not encouraging people to just walk away from their own personal income. We're just trying to be something in the middle that helps it be easier every single month.
<v Jordan Harris>因为我们查看统计数据，如果多数破产的人每月多有500美元，他们本可以避免破产。所以真的，如果我们能让大量的人每月赚500到600美元，那么对我们来说，这就是我们的甜点。我们不是在鼓励人们直接放弃他们个人的收入。我们只是试图成为中间的东西，帮助每个月变得更容易。

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:33.920
<v Brian Reed>Right, but from being at the conference, it just does seem like the messages are not that. They're not, we want to help you live a easier life every month with supplemental income and financial management.
<v Brian Reed>对，但从在会议上来看，信息似乎并非如此。它们不是，我们想通过补充收入和财务管理帮助你每个月过上更容易的生活。

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:49.450
<v Brian Reed>The message is much more like, your life is unsatisfactory. You are slave to some job. You don't have your own freedom. And this is the answer to that. The reality of the message I think people are getting is not what you just said. And does that concern you?
<v Brian Reed>信息更像是，你的生活是不令人满意的。你是某份工作的奴隶。你没有自己的自由。而这是对那个问题的答案。我认为人们得到的信息现实并非你刚才所说的。这让你担心吗？

00:21:49.450 --> 00:22:08.340
<v Jordan Harris>Yeah. We want to unify the message from corporate. Like I said, I think you guys caught us right in the spike of our growth where there were so many people that were involved in WakeUpNow that we didn't necessarily have as good as communication from corporate as we could have. But we're a different company than we were eight months ago.
<v Jordan Harris>是的。我们想统一来自公司的信息。就像我说的，我认为你们抓住了我们增长的尖峰，当时有太多人参与“唤醒现在”，以至于我们未必有来自公司尽可能好的沟通。但我们是一家与八个月前不同的公司。

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:19.340
<v Brian Reed>The conference we went to was in April. Jordan says the company had grown 4,000% over the previous year. And they were scrambling to deal with that expansion. WakeUpNow, by the way, was only founded in 2009.
<v Brian Reed>我们参加的会议是在四月。乔丹说公司在过去一年里增长了4000%。他们正忙于处理这种扩张。顺便说一下，“唤醒现在”成立于2009年。

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:43.480
<v Brian Reed>Since April, Jordan says they're running a tighter ship. They recently replaced their CEO. They're rewriting some of their introductory literature, downplaying the recruiting side of things. And they've changed what they say at their events. He says, WakeUpNow has never been intentionally vague. But he thinks we were not unique in our confusion. And that's one of the things they're trying to fix.
<v Brian Reed>自四月以来，乔丹说他们运营得更紧凑了。他们最近更换了首席执行官。他们正在重写一些介绍性资料，淡化招募方面的内容。而且他们改变了在活动中所说的内容。“唤醒现在”从未有意模糊，但他认为我们在困惑中并不独特。这也是他们试图解决的问题之一。

00:22:43.480 --> 00:23:02.590
<v Bianca Giaever>A couple weeks ago, we talked to a guy who studies companies like WakeUpNow, Amway and Herbalife and other network marketing companies. And he said, when reporters talk about them, they usually focus on the money people spend on them, but rarely discuss the part he considers more troubling and more destructive-- what it does to members' personal relationships.
<v Bianca Giaever>几周前，我们和一位研究像“唤醒现在”、安利和康宝莱等网络营销公司的研究人员交谈。他说，当记者谈论他们时，通常关注的是人们在他们身上花费的金钱，但很少讨论他认为更令人担忧和更具破坏性的部分——它对成员人际关系的影响。

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:12.610
<v Bianca Giaever>This guy's name is Robert L. Fitzpatrick. And he used a word to describe WakeUpNow that may sound hyperbolic. The word was "cult," except not a religious cult.
<v Bianca Giaever>这个人叫罗伯特·L·菲茨帕特里克（Robert L. Fitzpatrick）。他用了一个词来描述“唤醒现在”，这听起来可能有些夸张。这个词是“邪教”（cult），只不过不是宗教邪教。

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:20.920
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>It's an economic cult. That's the distinction that I think you may be grasping for when you say, what is this thing?
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>这是一个经济邪教。这是我想当你说“这是什么东西”时，你可能想抓到的区别。

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:43.980
<v Brian Reed>Robert Fitzpatrick says it's not an accident that it took us so long to figure out what WakeUpNow is, that the videos all seem so vague and the website was so confusing about pricing and products when we signed up as a member. He says, vagueness is part of a strategy companies use. Robert became interested in network marketing after he and some friends got caught up years ago in the frenzy of recruiting people into a gifting scheme.
<v Brian Reed>罗伯特·菲茨帕特里克说，我们花了这么长时间才弄明白“唤醒现在”是什么并非偶然，那些视频看起来都如此模糊，当我们注册成为会员时，网站在定价和产品上如此令人困惑。他说，模糊性是公司使用策略的一部分。罗伯特在多年前他和一些朋友陷入招募人们进入赠礼计划的狂热后，对网络营销产生了兴趣。

00:23:43.980 --> 00:24:00.070
<v Brian Reed>And he says the most disturbing thing about it was how it manipulated their thinking, made them suspend critical judgment. So he spent years researching how network marketing companies work. A regular business, Robert says, doesn't try to tell people how to treat their friends and family, which we saw at WakeUpNow.
<v Brian Reed>他说其中最令人不安的是它如何操纵他们的思维，让他们搁置批判性判断。所以他花了几年时间研究网络营销公司是如何运作的。罗伯特说，一家正规公司不会试图告诉人们如何对待他们的朋友和家人，而我们在“唤醒现在”看到了这一点。

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:11.890
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>The intention to isolate you from other people who are not in the scheme-- you are told to shun doubters, critics, even if they're in your family.
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>意图是将你与计划外的人隔离开来——你被告知要避开怀疑者、批评者，即使他们是你的家人。

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:18.370
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>And then, when you go around your friends and  they get uncomfortable, like, you buggin'! Guess what? You know who to walk away from.
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>然后，当你去拜访朋友，他们感到不舒服时，比如，你真是个麻烦！猜怎么着？你知道该避开谁了。

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:18.820
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[AFFIRMATIONS]
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[肯定语]

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:25.000
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>You know who to walk away from, because they can't understand your language. They won't be able to understand your language.
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>你知道该避开谁，因为他们听不懂你的语言。他们无法理解你的语言。

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:25.500
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[CHEERING]
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[欢呼]

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:29.770
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>Dream big. Dream big.
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>梦想远大。梦想远大。

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:45.220
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>So isolation-- and the other is a certain requirement of blind faith and obedience. You're not supposed to question. Critical thinking is for losers. You will see it when you believe it.
<v Robert L. Fitzpatrick>所以隔离——另一个是盲目信仰和服从的某种要求。你不应该质疑。批判性思维是给失败者准备的。你相信了，你就会看到它。

00:24:45.220 --> 00:25:03.230
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>This industry-- wrong. This busi-- no. This profession-- this profession rewards one thing. It rewards belief. How many of you believe you are Global sitting in that  chair right now?
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>这个行业——错。这个生意——不。这个职业——这个职业奖励一件事。它奖励信仰。现在坐在那张椅子上，你们有多少人相信你们是全球性的？

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:05.600
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[欢呼和掌声]

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:07.450
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[MIC DROPPING]
<v Wakeupnow Speaker>[丢掉麦克风]

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:14.430
<v Bianca Giaever>Global is the highest earning level in the company, the level See More Green is. That guy literally just dropped the mic, by the way.
<v Bianca Giaever>全球性是公司最高的收入级别，See More Green所在的级别。顺便说一下，那个家伙刚才真的扔掉了麦克风。

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:20.420
<v Bianca Giaever>[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v Bianca Giaever>[欢呼和掌声]

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:32.670
<v Brian Reed>We asked WakeUpNow's Chief Marketing Officer Jordan Harris if the company is a cult. And no surprise here. He says that's an unfair characterization. He says, he interprets those moments from the conference differently than Robert Fitzpatrick does.
<v Brian Reed>我们问“唤醒现在”的首席营销官乔丹·哈里斯公司是否是一个邪教。这并不意外。他说这是一个不公平的定性。他说，他对比罗伯特·菲茨帕特里克对会议中那些时刻的解读不同。

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:42.830
<v Brian Reed>WakeUpNow is not trying to isolate members from their family, he says. But if someone's being negative about your work, he thinks it is good advice not to hang around them. And what's wrong with believing in what you do?
<v Brian Reed>他说，“唤醒现在”并不是试图将成员与家人隔离开来。但如果有人对你的工作持负面态度，他认为不和他们交往是好建议。相信你所做的事情有什么错呢？

00:25:42.830 --> 00:26:07.730
<v Jordan Harris>Look, if you're going to do anything, you better believe that you can do before you start. I think that that is a universal truth. But to portray it as, the only way to be successful is to believe hard enough-- I would much rather have them preface that with, it is not enough to succeed. You've got to have the hard work. And you've got to have the talent to be successful.
<v Jordan Harris>听着，如果你要做任何事情，你最好在开始之前就相信你能做到。我认为那是一个普遍真理。但如果将其描绘成，成功的唯一途径就是足够努力地去相信——我宁愿他们在此之前加上，光有信仰是不够成功的。你必须有努力工作。而且你必须有才能才能成功。

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:27.730
<v Bianca Giaever>At the conference, everyone was so amped up. It was hard to get a sense of what it was really like to do WakeUpNow day in and day out. So we found someone who'd gone all in on WakeUpNow, who'd quit his job to dedicate himself to it, a guy with a wife and three young kids who recently stopped working in the kitchen of a really nice restaurant to do WakeUpNow.
<v Bianca Giaever>在会议上，每个人都非常亢奋。很难感知到日复一日做“唤醒现在”是什么感觉。所以我们找到了一个全身心投入“唤醒现在”、辞掉工作全身心投入其中的人，一个有妻子和三个小孩的男人，他最近停止在一家非常好的餐馆厨房工作来做“唤醒现在”。

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:50.620
<v Brian Reed>His name is Damien Lacks. He's 29 years old. We went to visit him at his apartment in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. Damien grew up in a rough part of Baltimore. He was a charismatic kid, a good athlete who did well in school. But he got into drugs, dropped out, did some time in jail. By the time he was 24, he was living in a halfway house in Gettysburg, where he moved to get away from the city, and working at a McDonald's.
<v Brian Reed>他叫达米恩·莱克斯（Damien Lacks）。他29岁。我们去宾夕法尼亚州葛底斯堡的公寓拜访了他。达米恩在巴尔的摩一个艰难的地方长大。他是一个有魅力的孩子，一个成绩优异的好运动员。但他开始吸毒，辍学，坐过牢。到24岁时，他住在葛底斯堡的一个中途宿舍里，为了远离城市而搬到那里，并在麦当劳工作。

00:26:50.620 --> 00:27:15.730
<v Damien Lacks>I didn't know what I wanted to do with life. At the same time though-- and this might sound strange to some people-- even back then, even since I was 10 years old, I've known that I was supposed to do something great. Now, I ain't saying it to boast. I just am giving praise to God. Because that's who he made me. I haven't always lived up to it. I've been terrible at living up to it.
<v Damien Lacks>我不知道我一生中想做什么。但与此同时——这对某些人来说可能听起来很奇怪——即使在当时，甚至从我10岁起，我就知道我本应该做一些伟大的事情。现在，我不是在吹嘘。我只是在赞美上帝。因为那是他创造我的样子。我不总是能做到。我在实现它方面一直很糟糕。

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:23.950
<v Bianca Giaever>After McDonald's, Damien worked in kitchens at a couple of pubs in town, then landed at a TGI Fridays. But Damien was bored there.
<v Bianca Giaever>在麦当劳之后，达米恩在镇上的几家酒吧厨房工作，然后落脚于TGI Fridays。但达米恩在那里很无聊。

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:41.710
<v Damien Lacks>The district manager would walk in. He wouldn't speak to the little guys on the line. I hated that feeling, feeling like I'm a little guy. And that the big guys, who are already making $100,000 a year, when you come around, you don't even have the decency to say, hey, how are you guys doing in here?
<v Damien Lacks>区域经理会走进来。他不会和流水线上的小人物说话。我讨厌那种感觉，感觉自己是个小人物。而那些已经年入10万美元的大人物，当你出现时，甚至没有礼貌说，嘿，你们在这里做得怎么样？

00:27:41.710 --> 00:28:02.210
<v Damien Lacks>You know, we hot. We sweating. We busting our ass. Excuse me. You don't really care about nothing but how the restaurant is running and how much money we're making. You don't care about the people at all. It makes you feel low. No, I hated that. I hated it.
<v Damien Lacks>你知道，我们很热。我们在流汗。我们拼命工作。对不起。你真的不在乎除了餐厅怎么运营以及我们赚了多少钱之外的任何事情。你根本不关心人。这让你感觉很卑微。不，我讨厌那样。我讨厌它。

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:21.070
<v Brian Reed>In other words, it was a J-O-B. So he applied to one of the best restaurants in the area, a place called Restaurant Sydney, founded by a chef he admired. Damien was taking online culinary classes. He had dreams of becoming a sous chef, of one day opening his own restaurant. So when Sidney hired him, he was thrilled.
<v Brian Reed>换句话说，这是一份J-O-B。所以他申请了该地区最好的餐馆之一，一家叫Restaurant Sydney的地方，由一位他敬佩的厨师创立。达米恩正在参加在线烹饪课程。他梦想成为一名副主厨，有朝一日开设自己的餐厅。所以当悉尼雇佣他时，他很激动。

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:23.920
<v Brian Reed>What were your expectations going in there?
<v Brian Reed>你进去时的期望是什么？

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:38.140
<v Damien Lacks>The highest level of chefness-- I don't know a better word. [LAUGH] And it wasn't all the way like that.
<v Damien Lacks>最高水平的厨师境界——我不知道更好的词。[笑] 情况并没有完全那样。

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:49.150
<v Bianca Giaever>When it came down to it, working in the kitchen at Restaurant Sidney was still just working in a kitchen. His co-workers grumbled, told offensive jokes to pass the time. They didn't seem inspired.
<v Bianca Giaever>当事情归结为，在Restaurant Sidney的厨房工作仍然只是在厨房工作。他的同事们抱怨，讲冒犯的笑话来打发时间。他们看起来并不受启发。

00:28:49.150 --> 00:29:00.700
<v Brian Reed>And Damien was only making $10 an hour. After babysitters and gas-- Sidney is 25 minutes from his house-- Damien says his pay was basically a wash. He kept asking for a raise, but wasn't getting one.
<v Brian Reed>而且达米恩每小时只赚10美元。付完保姆费和汽油钱——悉尼离他家有25分钟路程——达米恩说他的工资基本是白费。他一直要求加薪，但没有得到。

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:11.890
<v Bianca Giaever>And that's when an Instagram friend sent Damien some of the YouTube videos about WakeUpNow. Just like us, he says he'd never seen anything like them before. Unlike us, though, his reaction was--
<v Bianca Giaever>就在那时，一位Instagram朋友给达米恩发了一些关于“唤醒现在”的YouTube视频。就像我们一样，他说他以前从没见过这样的视频。然而，与我们不同的是，他的反应是——

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:20.860
<v Damien Lacks>The video she sent me was so inspiring. I went from being the lowest-paid person in my family to, now, the highest residual income-earner in my family.
<v Damien Lacks>她发给我的视频太鼓舞人心了。我从家里收入最低的人变成了现在家里最高的残余收入获得者。

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:35.370
<v Brian Reed>We asked Damien what about the video spoke to him. And what it came down to was the positivity. It was a bunch of young people and, in particular, young black people succeeding as entrepreneurs. They were working for themselves, in total control of their own destiny.
<v Brian Reed>我们问达米恩关于视频的什么打动了他。归结起来就是积极性。这是一群年轻人，特别是年轻的黑人，作为企业家成功了。他们为自己工作，完全掌控自己的命运。

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:36.620
<v Man 21>What are you doing in your job?
<v Man 21>你在工作中做什么？

00:29:36.620 --> 00:29:38.230
<v Man 21>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man 21>[笑]

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:57.490
<v Damien Lacks>To me, it was nostalgic. It took me back to that place, when I was a kid. You dream about where you want to be in your life. And it was like, that's right. That's right. That's what I wanted my life to be. That's right. I'm going after it. Thank you for reminding me. This is my calling right here.
<v Damien Lacks>对我来说，这很怀旧。它把我带回了小时候的那个地方。你梦想着你生活中想要到达的地方。就像是，没错。没错。那就是我想要的生活。没错。我正在追求它。谢谢你的提醒。这就是我的使命。

00:29:57.490 --> 00:30:20.290
<v Brian Reed>Damien began reading WakeUpNow's literature obsessively. He went on a vacation with his family. And he says, the whole time, he couldn't tear himself away. He compares it to reading scripture. The company's Quick Start Guide includes a whole section called "Your Thoughts can Change the Future." Damien loved that. It talks about feeding yourself positive ideas and filtering out the negative, not hanging around negative people.
<v Brian Reed>达米恩开始痴迷地阅读“唤醒现在”的文献。他带着家人去度假。他说，整段时间，他都无法把自己从这上面拉开。他把它比作阅读经文。公司的“快速入门指南”包含了一个整章，叫“你的思想可以改变未来”。达米恩喜欢那个。它谈论给自己喂养积极的想法，过滤掉消极的想法，不和消极的人在一起。

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:42.330
<v Brian Reed>After that, every dirty joke his co-workers made felt like it was poisoning his mind. Then, after weeks of asking for a raise, his boss told him he wouldn't be getting one. Not long after, Damien quit Restaurant Sidney and devoted himself to WakeUpNow.
<v Brian Reed>在那之后，他的同事讲的每一个脏笑话都让他觉得是在毒害他的思想。然后，在要求加薪数周后，他的老板告诉他他不会得到加薪。没过多久，达米恩辞去了Restaurant Sidney的工作，全身心投入到“唤醒现在”中。

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:59.260
<v Bianca Giaever>When we visited Damien, he'd been doing WakeUpNow for four months. Here's what a typical work day looks like for him. He wakes up and helps get his two boys off to school. And once they're off, he usually dials into a 10:00 AM motivational group call. But most of the day, he watches his baby daughter.
<v Bianca Giaever>当我们拜访达米恩时，他做“唤醒现在”已经四个月了。这是他典型的工作日。他醒来并帮助他的两个孩子去学校。一旦他们走了，他通常会拨打上午10点的励志小组电话。但大部分时间，他照顾他的女婴。

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:19.850
<v Bianca Giaever>While she's napping, he spends a few hours poking around on the computer, watching YouTube videos about how to present WakeUpNow to people and, then, just kind of being on Facebook. If he starts to feel down, he'll watch an uplifting video. Early on, he tried calling every contact in his phone to tell them about WakeUpNow. That yielded exactly one WakeUpNow recruit.
<v Bianca Giaever>当她午睡时，他花几个小时在电脑上摸索，观看关于如何向人们展示“唤醒现在”的YouTube视频，然后，只是在Facebook上。如果他开始感到情绪低落，他会观看令人振奋的视频。早些时候，他试图给手机里的每个联系人打电话告诉他们关于“唤醒现在”的事。这产生了正好一个“唤醒现在”的新成员。

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:21.500
<v Damien Lacks>My mom.
<v Damien Lacks>我的妈妈。

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:31.700
<v Bianca Giaever>His mom. After four months, she's still the only recruit he has, which means he's not making any income. And in fact, he's paying $100 a month to be part of WakeUpNow.
<v Bianca Giaever>他的妈妈。四个月后，她仍然是他拥有的唯一成员，这意味着他没有任何收入。事实上，他每月支付100美元作为“唤醒现在”的一部分。

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:40.760
<v Bianca Giaever>What are you going to do if another four months go by and you still haven't gotten anyone and aren't making any money? And you're spending money to be in it?
<v Bianca Giaever>如果你再过四个月还是没招募到任何人，也没赚到钱怎么办？而你还在花钱加入它？

00:31:40.760 --> 00:31:52.550
<v Damien Lacks>Um-- that's a hard question. Because I honestly know that it's going to work.
<v Damien Lacks>嗯——那是个很难的问题。因为我诚实地知道它会成功。

00:31:52.550 --> 00:32:00.800
<v Brian Reed>We spent a lot of time debating with Damien about this. It was nearly impossible to get him to even entertain the hypothetical of WakeUpNow not working.
<v Brian Reed>我们花了很多时间与达米恩辩论这个问题。几乎不可能让他甚至考虑“唤醒现在”不成功这种假设。

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:06.590
<v Brian Reed>If the year goes by and you haven't made any money with WakeUpNow or network marketing, would you be disappointed?
<v Brian Reed>如果一年过去了，你还没赚到钱，无论通过“唤醒现在”还是网络营销，你会失望吗？

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:09.380
<v Damien Lacks>No, I would just keep going.
<v Damien Lacks>不，我会继续做下去。

00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:15.320
<v Brian Reed>Think about that. A year has gone by with no success. You wouldn't feel any kind of way about that?
<v Brian Reed>想一想。一年过去了，没有任何成功。你对此不会有什么感觉吗？

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:27.380
<v Damien Lacks>A year gone by-- and network marketing is successful. Think about doctors and lawyers. They go to school for four years, eight years, before they actually reach the time when they can get paid for what they've been training for.
<v Damien Lacks>一年过去了——网络营销是成功的。想想医生和律师。他们上学四年、八年，直到他们真正到达可以为他们训练的东西获得报酬的时候。

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:39.860
<v Brian Reed>Damien knows the stats, that just 1% of WakeUpNow members make a living. He actually pointed the numbers out to us on his own. But it's almost as if those dismal statistics made Damien even more optimistic about his prospects.
<v Brian Reed>达米恩知道统计数据，只有1%的“唤醒现在”成员以此为生。他实际上自己向我们指出了这些数字。但几乎就好像那些惨淡的统计数据让达米恩对自己的前景更加乐观。

00:32:39.860 --> 00:33:02.550
<v Damien Lacks>I have the persistence to become in the 1%, in the less than 1%. Because it says, the results are not typical. Well, I say, I'm not typical. And the thing about it is, more people could be successful. But more people don't have desire. They're not going to put in the work. And if you tell yourself it's too hard, it's going to be too hard.
<v Damien Lacks>我有毅力成为那1%中的一员，不到1%。因为它说，结果不是典型的。嗯，我说，我不典型。关于这一点，更多的人本来可以成功的。但更多人没有欲望。他们不会投入工作。如果你告诉自己这太难了，它就会太难。

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:17.150
<v Brian Reed>But what if it's not the fact that you're telling yourself that something is really hard, but that it just is really hard? Like, that is the reality, the chances of making money are low. And so, when someone's telling themself that it's hard, they're right.
<v Brian Reed>但如果事实不是你告诉自己某事很难，而它确实很难呢？就像，那是现实，赚钱的机会很低。所以，当有人告诉自己这很难时，他们是对的。

00:33:17.150 --> 00:33:23.380
<v Damien Lacks>It's only hard if you believe it's hard.
<v Damien Lacks>只有当你相信它很难时，它才难。

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:47.610
<v Bianca Giaever>We thought that one person who might be silently freaking out about this was Damien's wife, Candice. Candace has a full-time job managing a store at the nearby outlet mall and is now the sole breadwinner in their family of five. They have two kids, a baby and an eight-year-old, and just became the legal guardians of a third child, a 12-year-old who neither is related to, the son of one of Damien's ex-girlfriends.
<v Bianca Giaever>我们以为可能默默对这一点感到恐慌的一个人是达米恩的妻子坎迪斯（Candice）。坎迪斯在附近的奥特莱斯购物中心管理一家店，拥有全职工作，现在是他们五口之家的唯一养家糊口的人。他们有两个孩子，一个婴儿和一个八岁孩子，刚成为第三个孩子的合法监护人，一个12岁的孩子，两人都没有血缘关系，是达米恩前女友之一的儿子。

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:58.560
<v Bianca Giaever>So we took Candice aside, out of Damian's earshot, to ask how she was dealing with the pressure of providing for three kids while Damien didn't have a job and wasn't bringing in any money with WakeUpNow.
<v Bianca Giaever>所以我们把坎迪斯带到一边，在达米恩听不到的地方，问她如何在达米恩没有工作且没有通过“唤醒现在”赚到钱的情况下，应对抚养三个孩子的压力。

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:14.760
<v Candice Lacks>I'm not as stressed out. I have a lot more help around the house. So my days off, I actually can enjoy instead of having mounds of laundry to do. [LAUGHS] So like, it's really helped us as a family unit.
<v Candice Lacks>我没有那么大的压力。我在家里得到了很多帮助。所以我的休息日，我实际上可以享受，而不是有成堆的衣服要洗。[笑] 所以这确实帮助了我们作为一个家庭单位。

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:37.960
<v Brian Reed>This is not the answer we were expecting. But Candice likes her job. And she says that her income is enough to support the family without Damien earning money. She says they're not in financial trouble at all. When Damien had restaurant gigs where he worked long erratic hours, she and the kids would sometimes go days without seeing him. Now that Damien has left the restaurant industry for WakeUpNow, Candice says her kids get to be raised by their father.
<v Brian Reed>这不是我们期待的答案。但坎迪斯喜欢她的工作。她说她的收入足以支撑这个家庭，无需达米恩赚钱。她说他们根本没有陷入财务困境。当达米恩在餐馆打工时，工作时间很长且不稳定，她和孩子们有时会几天看不到他。现在达米恩为了“唤醒现在”离开了餐馆业，坎迪斯说她的孩子们可以由他们的父亲抚养。

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:56.560
<v Candice Lacks>And it's great. Because, with us adding on another child, getting that child in a routine and working on his homework-- because he is behind, so he needs a lot more help and attention with schoolwork and stuff. It's beneficial right now with the dynamics of our family.
<v Candice Lacks>这太棒了。因为，随着我们增加另一个孩子，让那个孩子进入常规并做家庭作业——因为他落后了，所以他在学校作业等方面需要更多的帮助和关注。这对我们家庭的动态有益。

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:58.230
<v Brian Reed>And Damien's been able to do that with him?
<v Brian Reed>达米恩能和他一起做那些吗？

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:09.210
<v Candice Lacks>Absolutely. He cooks. He does homework. He cleans. So I'm very thankful for that.
<v Candice Lacks>绝对。他做饭。他辅导作业。他清洁。所以我非常感激。

