<v Ira Glass>Bianca Giaever and Brian Reed. A few weeks after the visit, Damien-- he decided to put WakeUpNow on pause. He is no longer a member. He says he's not paying money every month. He wanted to make some money for the holidays, went back to work at TGI Fridays where he's now a waiter, which says he likes a lot more than working in the kitchen.
<v Ira Glass>比安卡·贾维尔和布莱恩·里德。在拜访几周后，达米恩——他决定暂停“唤醒现在”。他不再是成员了。他说他没有每月付钱。他想为节日赚些钱，回到了TGI Fridays工作，现在是一名服务员，他说这比在厨房工作让他喜欢得多。

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:44.090
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a rich guy tries to wake up other rich guys about the issue of how rich certain rich guys are getting. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个有钱人试图唤醒其他有钱人，关于某些有钱人变得多有钱的问题。一分钟后，当我们的节目在芝加哥公共广播电台继续时。

00:36:44.090 --> 00:37:05.180
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme. Today, with people making resolutions for how this new year will be different from the previous one, our show is "Wake Up Now." I hope you notice how classy we're keeping it here on the show by not inserting alarm clock sounds every time I say those words. Wake Up Now-- stories of people trying to wake themselves up or wake up others.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。当然，我们每周在节目中选择一个主题。今天，随着人们为新的一年将如何与过去不同做出决定，我们的节目是“唤醒现在”。我希望你注意到我们在节目中保持得多么高级，而不是每次我说这些词时都插入闹钟声。“唤醒现在”——关于人们试图唤醒自己或唤醒他人的故事。

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:32.370
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act 2 of our program, Act 2, Board Games. So there's this thing that comes up in the news pretty regularly, CEO pay. When it comes up, rarely is it good for the CEOs. Usually, the point is that CEOs are overpaid, something needs to be done. If you figure that most people work 40 or 50 years in their work lives, it would take a typical worker four or five lifetimes to earn what the CEOs of large companies earn in a single year.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了我们节目的第二幕，第二幕，棋盘游戏。所以新闻中经常出现一件事，首席执行官薪酬。当它出现时，这对首席执行官来说很少是好事。通常重点是首席执行官报酬过高，需要做点什么。如果你算上大多数人在工作生活中工作40或50年，普通工人需要四或五辈子才能赚到大公司首席执行官一年赚的钱。

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:50.720
<v Ira Glass>So you hear statistics like that in these stories. But what you almost never hear is anybody actually trying to cut the pay of a CEO. Well, David Kestenbaum and Jacob Goldstein of NPR'S "Planet Money" found a guy who tried to wake people up to the fact of the CEO of one small company seemed to be making way too much money.
<v Ira Glass>所以你在这些故事中听到了这样的统计数据。但你几乎从没听说有人试图削减首席执行官的薪水。嗯，NPR《货币星球》（Planet Money）的戴维·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）和雅各布·戈德斯坦（Jacob Goldstein）发现了一个试图唤醒人们的事实的人，即一家小公司的首席执行官似乎赚得太多了。

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:59.600
<v David Kestenbaum>It takes an unusual person to wage a war against a CEO and his pay. The odds of success are just so low. And Tim Stabosz was definitely an outsider.
<v David Kestenbaum>需要一个不寻常的人才能对首席执行官及其薪酬发动战争。成功的几率太低了。而蒂姆·斯塔博斯（Tim Stabosz）绝对是个局外人。

