<v David Kestenbaum>There are a lot of calls like this.
<v David Kestenbaum>有很多像这样的电话。

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:46.210
<v Jacob Goldstein>A CEO's salary is set by a company's board of directors. And Shapiro suspected Richard Horowitz had packed the board with his cronies, people who paid him more than he deserved. So Andrew hired a private detective and did some digging himself. He discovered that, in fact, several board members played golf at the same country club as the CEO.
<v Jacob Goldstein>首席执行官的薪水由公司的董事会设定。夏皮罗怀疑理查德·霍洛维茨用他的亲信填满了董事会，那些付给他比他应得更多的钱的人。所以安德鲁雇了一名私家侦探并自己进行了挖掘。他发现，事实上，几名董事会成员与首席执行官在同一个乡村俱乐部打高尔夫球。

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:54.280
<v Andrew Shapiro>The Glen Oaks Country Club of Westbury, New York. Glen Oaks Country Club of Old Westbury, New York.
<v Andrew Shapiro>纽约州韦斯特伯里的格伦奥克斯乡村俱乐部（Glen Oaks Country Club of Westbury, New York）。纽约州旧韦斯特伯里的格伦奥克斯乡村俱乐部。

00:44:54.280 --> 00:45:09.190
<v David Kestenbaum>Nothing illegal or maybe even surprising about these guys blowing into the same country club. But still, what would General Colin Powell do? He'd get them off the board. Andrew suggested several potential new board members. And Tim, Tim nominated himself.
<v David Kestenbaum>关于这些人吹嘘同一个乡村俱乐部并没有什么违法，甚至也许并不令人惊讶。但仍然，科林·鲍威尔（Colin Powell）将军会做什么？他会把他们踢出董事会。安德鲁建议了几位潜在的新董事会成员。而蒂姆，蒂姆提名了他自己。

00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:20.710
<v Jacob Goldstein>Getting new people on a board is tough. There is a vote. But in most cases, the existing board members get to decide who's on the ballot. It's as if members of Congress were the ones who decided who got to run for Congress.
<v Jacob Goldstein>让新的人进入董事会很难。有一个投票。但在大多数情况下，现任董事会成员可以决定谁在选票上。这就好比国会议员决定谁可以竞选国会议员。

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:31.330
<v David Kestenbaum>So Tim and Andrew went to the company's annual meeting to try to convince the board to put their candidates on the ballot. It was the first time Andrew Shapiro and Richard Horowitz, the CEO, had met in person.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以蒂姆和安德鲁参加了公司的年度会议，试图说服董事会把他们的候选人放在选票上。这是安德鲁·夏皮罗和理查德·霍洛维茨第一次亲自见面。

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:43.930
<v Andrew Shapiro>It actually was quite crowded. Every seat at the table was full. And there was the paid-for advisers standing around the periphery of the table, in the room, but along the wall.
<v Andrew Shapiro>它实际上相当拥挤。桌子上的每个座位都满了。在桌子的外围，房间里，但在墙边，有付费的顾问站着。

00:45:43.930 --> 00:45:45.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Where was the CEO?
<v David Kestenbaum>首席执行官在哪里？

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:48.070
<v Andrew Shapiro>Oh, he's sitting directly across the table from me.
<v Andrew Shapiro>噢，他正坐在我对面。

00:45:48.070 --> 00:45:50.680
<v David Kestenbaum>Oh, it must be awkward. What's it like?
<v David Kestenbaum>噢，那一定很尴尬。那是什么感觉？

00:45:50.680 --> 00:46:17.320
<v Andrew Shapiro>Oh, it's not awkward or not. I go into these rooms with the distinct view that I am an owner of this business and you members of the board of directors are my agents. And you work for me. You don't work for Mr. Horowitz. You don't work for management. Management works for you, the board. And the board, you work for me and all of the other shareholders.
<v Andrew Shapiro>噢，没什么尴尬不尴尬的。我走进这些房间，有明确的观点，我是这项业务的所有者，而你们董事会成员是我的代理人。你们为我工作。你们不为霍洛维茨先生工作。你们不为管理层工作。管理层为您（董事会）工作。而董事会，你们为我和所有其他股东工作。

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:37.540
<v David Kestenbaum>The board did not seem immediately swayed by this argument. And it's easy to imagine how this whole thing might feel on the other side if you're, say, Richard Horowitz, the CEO; if you've got two guys who have never even worked in the tool business telling you how they think the business that your father built up should be run and how much you should be paid and who should be on your board.
<v David Kestenbaum>董事会似乎并没有立即被这个论点所左右。很容易想象如果如果你是理查德·霍洛维茨，首席执行官，如果你有两个从未在工具行业工作过的人告诉你他们认为你父亲建立的业务应该如何经营，你应该拿多少报酬，谁应该在你的董事会里，这一切在另一方会是什么感觉。

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:53.530
<v Jacob Goldstein>For Tim Stabosz, what had started out as a rational investment, something just based on numbers, had become emotional, had become something hard to let go of. At some point in this whole fight, Tim's letters to the company took on a different tone. He got rid of the lawyer who'd been vetting them.
<v Jacob Goldstein>对于蒂姆·斯塔博斯来说，开始作为一个理性的投资，仅仅基于数字的东西，已经变得情绪化了，已经变得难以放手。在这个战斗的某个时刻，蒂姆给公司的信件带上了不同的语调。他解雇了一直审查它们的律师。

00:46:53.530 --> 00:47:09.980
<v Jacob Goldstein>Some of the letters start to have words in all caps. Some start to feel weirdly intimate. One is addressed just to Richard and uses phrases like, "I was at the end of my rope," and, "goodness knows, I tried a different course."
<v Jacob Goldstein>有些信件开始有全部大写的单词。有些开始感到奇怪地亲密。一封只写给理查德，使用诸如“我到了我的绳子尽头”和“上帝知道，我尝试了不同的课程”之类的短语。

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:26.130
<v David Kestenbaum>And then, in the summer of 2010, more than a decade after Tim first bought stock in P&F Industries, something finally went his way. There was a change on the board. The two guys who played golf at the same country club as the CEO stepped down from the committee that set the CEO's pay.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后，在2010年夏天，在蒂姆第一次买P&F工业股票十多年后，事情终于朝着他的方向发展了。董事会发生了变化。那两个与首席执行官在同一个乡村俱乐部打高尔夫球的人从设定首席执行官薪酬的委员会中卸任了。

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:31.300
<v Jacob Goldstein>Also, one of the people Andrew had recommended for the board got put on the ballot and elected.
<v Jacob Goldstein>此外，安德鲁推荐进入董事会的人之一被列入选票并当选了。

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:53.130
<v David Kestenbaum>And a year and a half later-- if you haven't noticed, this is an incredibly long process-- it finally happened, the thing that almost never happens. The board of P&F did what Andrew and Tim wanted. They cut Richard Horowitz's base salary by a lot, from $975,000 to $650,000, a cut of over $300,000.
<v David Kestenbaum>一年半后——如果你没注意到，这是一个极其漫长的过程——它终于发生了，几乎从未发生过的事情。P&F董事会做了安德鲁和蒂姆想做的事。他们大幅削减了理查德·霍洛维茨的基本薪水，从97.5万美元降至65万美元，削减了超过30万美元。

00:47:53.130 --> 00:48:07.890
<v Jacob Goldstein>It actually made the news, at least a little corner of the news, an article in a publication called "Institutional Investor." An expert in that article said it was the first time he'd ever seen shareholders get a board to cut the pay of a sitting CEO.
<v Jacob Goldstein>它实际上成了新闻，至少是新闻的一角，一份名为《机构投资者》（Institutional Investor）的刊物上的一篇文章。那篇文章中的一位专家说，这是他见过的第一次股东让董事会削减在任首席执行官的薪水。

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:11.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Tim Stabosz, of course, was elated about the pay cut.
<v David Kestenbaum>蒂姆·斯塔博斯当然对减薪感到欣喜若狂。

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:19.950
<v Tim Stabosz>Well, it felt fantastic. It felt great. It felt like I had served to effect this change, that I was an agent of change.
<v Tim Stabosz>嗯，这感觉很棒。感觉很好。感觉我为促成这种改变提供了服务，我是变革的代理人。

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:35.520
<v Jacob Goldstein>That feeling did not last. It turns out, that new contract also made it easier for Richard Horowitz to get a bigger bonus. And in 2012, even though Horowitz's base pay had been cut, he actually ended up making more money than he had the previous year.
<v Jacob Goldstein>那种感觉并没有持续。事实证明，那份新合同也让理查德·霍洛维茨更容易获得更大的奖金。在2012年，尽管霍洛维茨的基本薪水被削减了，他实际上最终比前一年赚了更多的钱。

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:41.960
<v David Kestenbaum>And then, earlier this year, Richard Horowitz got a raise. The board boosted his base pay by $50,000.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后，今年早些时候，理查德·霍洛维茨加薪了。董事会将他的基本工资提高了5万美元。

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:46.860
<v Tim Stabosz>And they also raised the bonus percentages, so he would be able to earn even more on the bonus.
<v Tim Stabosz>他们还提高了奖金百分比，这样他就能在奖金上赚得更多。

00:48:46.860 --> 00:49:04.500
<v David Kestenbaum>The company only let us talk to one person on its side of things. But it was someone we definitely wanted to hear from, the board member that Andrew Shapiro had recommended. We met him one afternoon at a business club at the top of a tall building in Philadelphia. His name is Howard Brownstein. And he told us he thinks Richard Horowitz's pay is reasonable.
<v David Kestenbaum>公司只让我们和他们一边的一人交谈。但这绝对是我们想听的人，安德鲁·夏皮罗推荐的那位董事会成员。我们在费城一栋高楼顶部的商业俱乐部见到了他。他叫霍华德·布朗斯坦（Howard Brownstein）。他告诉我们他认为理查德·霍洛维茨的薪酬是合理的。

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:20.760
<v Howard Brownstein>Yeah, I would say to you that, based on the work that the comp committee did, as they reported it to us and as we listened to it, that the compensation is within the realm of reasonableness for our company and how it's doing and the money we make for the shareholders.
<v Howard Brownstein>是的，我会告诉你，根据薪酬委员会所做的工作，正如他们向我们报告的那样，正如我们所听到的那样，薪酬在我们公司及其实际表现以及我们为股东赚的钱的合理范围内。

00:49:20.760 --> 00:49:46.320
<v Jacob Goldstein>We asked Brownstein about that compensation study that Tim asked about on the conference call, the study that compares Richard Horowitz's pay to the CEOs of other companies. And we read Brownstein this letter Tim wrote, asking the board to release details from the report. "Asked" is probably the wrong word. Tim writes, I demand, in all caps. And then, at the end, also in caps, I have waited long enough. I am done waiting.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我们问布朗斯坦关于蒂姆在电话会议上问的薪酬研究，那个比较理查德·霍洛维茨薪水与其他公司首席执行官薪水的研究。我们读给布朗斯坦蒂姆写的那封要求董事会发布报告细节的信。“要求”大概是错误的词。蒂姆写道，我要求（Demand），全部大写。然后，最后，也用大写，我已经等得够久了。我不想再等了。

00:49:46.320 --> 00:50:10.590
<v Howard Brownstein>Well, Mr. Stabosz obviously hasn't been able to find the Caps Lock key on his typewriter. Look, generally in my world, when somebody demands something, it's because they have a legal right to it. He may say he demands it, but the fact is, he doesn't have a right to it. So what he really meant when he said, I demand it, is I really, really want it. I get it. It's OK. You can't have it. Next.
<v Howard Brownstein>嗯，斯塔博斯先生显然还没能在他的打字机上找到大写锁定键。看，通常在我的世界里，当某人要求某事时，是因为他们拥有法律权利。他说他要求它，但事实是，他没有权利拥有它。所以当他说“我要求它”时，他真正意思是，我真的、真的非常想要它。我懂。没关系。你不能拥有它。下一个。

00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:12.660
<v Jacob Goldstein>He's one of the owners of the company, right?
<v Jacob Goldstein>他是公司的所有者之一，对吧？

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:22.170
<v Howard Brownstein>Sure he is. But the owners of the company only have the rights that they have, the rights the law gives them. It doesn't give them more than that.
<v Howard Brownstein>当然他是。但公司的所有者只拥有他们拥有的权利，法律赋予他们的权利。它不给他们超出此范围的权利。

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:33.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Since P&F wouldn't release that study, we asked a company that analyzes CEO compensation to take a look. The company is called Equilar. They do a lot of work for companies who are trying to figure out how much to pay their CEOs.
<v David Kestenbaum>因为P&F不会发布那份研究，我们要求一家分析首席执行官薪酬的公司看一看。这家公司叫Equilar。他们为试图弄清楚支付多少薪水给首席执行官的公司做了很多工作。

00:50:33.600 --> 00:50:56.700
<v Jacob Goldstein>Here is what Equilar found. Among roughly 200 companies of similar size, Richard Horowitz's pay ranked near the very top, in the top 7%, more than twice the average. Equilar also looked at a smaller group of companies that, like P&F, are in what's called the "industrial goods" sector. There were 13 companies in that group. The top paid CEO was Richard Horowitz.
<v Jacob Goldstein>这就是Equilar发现的。在大约200家类似规模的公司中，理查德·霍洛维茨的薪水排在几乎顶端，前7%，平均值的两倍多。Equilar还查看了另一组较小的公司，它们像P&F一样，属于所谓的“工业品”领域。该组中有13家公司。报酬最高的首席执行官是理查德·霍洛维茨。

00:50:56.700 --> 00:51:10.050
<v David Kestenbaum>In other words, according to the study, Richard Horowitz is one of the highest paid or the highest paid CEO for what he does.
<v David Kestenbaum>换句话说，根据该研究，理查德·霍洛维茨是同类工作中报酬最高或最高的首席执行官。

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:19.650
<v Jacob Goldstein>Tim Stabosz lost. But he had owned shares in P&F for most of his adult life. And when you've been in something that long, it can be hard to know when to walk away.
<v Jacob Goldstein>蒂姆·斯塔博斯输了。但他拥有P&F的股份几乎是他成年后的大部分时间。当你做某件事这么久时，可能很难知道什么时候走开。

00:51:19.650 --> 00:51:22.420
<v David Kestenbaum>What happens next? Are you going to keep at this?
<v David Kestenbaum>接下来会发生什么？你还要继续这样做吗？

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:35.450
<v Tim Stabosz>That's a good question. I hadn't thought about that. Um, yeah, yeah. I want the company to know that I'm not going anywhere.
<v Tim Stabosz>这是个好问题。我还没想过那个。嗯，是的，是的。我想让公司知道我哪儿也不去。

00:51:35.450 --> 00:51:39.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you ever think about giving up?
<v David Kestenbaum>你想过放弃吗？

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:42.160
<v Tim Stabosz>Um, no.
<v Tim Stabosz>嗯，不。

00:51:42.160 --> 00:51:42.550
<v David Kestenbaum>Really?
<v David Kestenbaum>真的吗？

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:45.520
<v Tim Stabosz>Well, I mean, I'm trying to think about what giving up means.
<v Tim Stabosz>嗯，我的意思是，我试图思考放弃意味着什么。

00:51:45.520 --> 00:52:00.160
<v David Kestenbaum>I can tell you what giving up looks like. After we talked to Tim this summer, he had a momentary cash crunch. He had to sell all his shares. The company bought them from him. And as part of the deal, Tim promised not to buy any shares of P&F Industries for three years.
<v David Kestenbaum>我可以告诉你放弃看起来像什么。在今年夏天我们和蒂姆交谈后，他经历了短暂的现金紧缩。他不得不卖掉所有股份。公司从他那里买下了它们。作为交易的一部分，蒂姆承诺三年内不购买任何P&F工业的股票。

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:22.230
<v Jacob Goldstein>It turns out it's really hard to cut a CEO's pay, even if you own 5% of the company. Now, he has even less power to do anything about it. Now, Tim Stabosz is just like everybody else.
<v Jacob Goldstein>事实证明，要削减首席执行官的薪水真的很难，即使你拥有公司的5%。现在，他在处理这件事上拥有的权力更少了。现在，蒂姆·斯塔博斯就像其他人一样。

00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:34.470
<v Ira Glass>Jacob Goldstein and David Kestenbaum from the NPR podcast "Planet Money," the very entertaining podcast about economics. Get it for free at npr.org/money or whenever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>雅各布·戈德斯坦和戴维·克斯滕鲍姆来自NPR播客《货币星球》，关于经济的非常有趣的播客。在npr.org/money或任何你获得播客的地方免费获取它。

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:55.920
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "BIG BOSS MAN" BY KOKO TAYLOR]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “大老板”（Big Boss Man） - 科科·泰勒（Koko Taylor）]

00:52:55.920 --> 00:53:22.410
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, Sleep No More. OK, so to end today's program about waking up, let's leave the world of metaphor completely. So we're not talking about waking up to a new way of seeing the world or waking up to the things you need to do to change your life or waking up other people to the issue of overpaid CEOs, or whatever issue. I'm talking about just waking up, just waking up, pure and simple. That shouldn't be hard, right? You do it every day.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，永不入眠。好吧，所以为了结束今天关于醒来的节目，让我们完全离开隐喻的世界。所以我们不是在谈论醒来迎接看待世界的新方式，或者醒来处理你需要做的改变生活的事情，或者唤醒其他人关注报酬过高的首席执行官问题，无论什么问题。我是在谈论只是醒来，只是醒来，纯粹而简单。那不应该很难，对吧？你每天都这样做。

00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:25.500
<v Ira Glass>[ALARM BEEPING]
<v Ira Glass>[闹钟哔哔声]

00:53:25.500 --> 00:53:35.500
<v Ira Glass>Springfield, Oregon, Monday morning during finals week, 7:30. Morgan Peach, a grad student hears the alarm that's supposed to wake him up. And he shuts it off. This happens again at 7:35.
<v Ira Glass>俄勒冈州斯普林菲尔德，期末周周一早上，7:30。莫根·皮奇（Morgan Peach），一名研究生听到了本该叫醒他的闹钟。他关掉了它。这在7:35再次发生。

00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:36.950
<v Ira Glass>[ALARM BEEPING]
<v Ira Glass>[闹钟哔哔声]

00:53:36.950 --> 00:53:38.380
<v Ira Glass>And again at 7:40.
<v Ira Glass>然后在7:40又一次。

00:53:38.380 --> 00:53:39.370
<v Morgan Peach>[MOANING]
<v Morgan Peach>[呻吟声]

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:42.580
<v Morgan Peach>[ALARM BEEPING]
<v Morgan Peach>[闹钟哔哔声]

00:53:42.580 --> 00:53:44.000
<v Ira Glass>Once more at 7:45.
<v Ira Glass>在7:45再来一次。

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:47.140
<v Ira Glass>[ALARM BEEPING]
<v Ira Glass>[闹钟哔哔声]

00:53:47.140 --> 00:53:54.070
<v Ira Glass>You get the idea, 8:15, 8:25. Finally, 9 o'clock, his girlfriend, Angela Evancie, who made this recording, steps in.
<v Ira Glass>你明白了，8:15，8:25。最后，9点钟，制作此录音的女朋友安吉拉·埃万西（Angela Evancie）介入了。

00:53:54.070 --> 00:53:56.440
<v Morgan Peach>[DEEP INHALE] It's time.
<v Morgan Peach>[深吸气] 是时候了。

00:53:56.440 --> 00:53:57.490
<v Angela Evancie>It's time.
<v Angela Evancie>是时候了。

00:53:57.490 --> 00:53:58.920
<v Morgan Peach>It's time.
<v Morgan Peach>是时候了。

00:53:58.920 --> 00:54:16.220
<v Ira Glass>Right. He doesn't get up. Besides an exam, he has two research papers to turn in and 55 exams and papers to grade. Angela sees him struggle everyday with waking up. But she thought, this week-- this week-- for once, he would do it when the alarm sounds, because he has so much to do.
<v Ira Glass>对。他没有起来。除了考试，他还有两篇研究论文要交，以及55场考试和论文要打分。安吉拉每天看到他为醒来而挣扎。但她以为，这周——这周——这一次，当闹钟响起时他会起来的，因为他有很多事要做。

00:54:16.220 --> 00:54:29.460
<v Ira Glass>Tuesday, it goes off first at 7:30, then seven more times. 7:40, 7:45, 8 o'clock, 8:05, 8:10, 8:15, 8:20. Wednesday, pretty much the same deal. At some point, Angela says to her sleeping boyfriend--
<v Ira Glass>周二，它首先在7:30响起，然后又响了七次。7:40，7:45，8点，8:05，8:10，8:15，8:20。周三，基本情况一样。在某个时候，安吉拉对她睡觉的男朋友说——

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:32.200
<v Angela Evancie>You set the alarm for 9 o'clock?
<v Angela Evancie>你设闹钟到9点了吗？

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:36.150
<v Morgan Peach>Yes, I did.
<v Morgan Peach>是的，我设了。

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:39.570
<v Angela Evancie>Can you afford to sleep in that late?
<v Angela Evancie>你睡得起那么晚吗？

00:54:39.570 --> 00:54:45.610
<v Morgan Peach>Certainly can. I'm wealthy in time, not in money.
<v Morgan Peach>当然可以。我的时间很富有，钱不富有。

00:54:45.610 --> 00:54:56.460
<v Ira Glass>People can be surprisingly articulate when they're barely awake sometimes. Wednesday night, Morgan tells Angela that the next morning, Thursday, he actually is going to have to get up. The geography paper's due at noon.
<v Ira Glass>人们有时在几乎醒着的时候可以惊人地表达清楚。周三晚上，莫根告诉安吉拉，第二天早上，周四，他实际上不得不起来。地理论文中午截止。

00:54:56.460 --> 00:55:07.480
<v Morgan Peach>Yeah, could you set the alarm for 7:30 tomorrow? I'd like to get up at 7:30 and work on my research papers and finish them.
<v Morgan Peach>是的，你能把明天闹钟设到7:30吗？我想在7:30起来，研究我的研究论文并完成它们。

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:08.860
<v Ira Glass>So next morning.
<v Ira Glass>所以第二天早上。

00:55:08.860 --> 00:55:23.650
<v Angela Evancie>(WHISPERING) And now it's 8:00. The alarm went off. And he reset it for 8:30. I really thought he was going to do it this morning.
<v Angela Evancie>（低语）现在8点钟了。闹钟响了。他把它重置到8:30。我真的以为他今天早上会做的。

00:55:23.650 --> 00:55:27.750
<v Ira Glass>Sometime near 10:00 AM, they finally have this conversation.
<v Ira Glass>大约上午10点某时，他们终于有了这个对话。

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:28.860
<v Angela Evancie>It's time to get up.
<v Angela Evancie>该起床了。

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:32.120
<v Morgan Peach>[YAWN] That's what you always say.
<v Morgan Peach>[哈欠] 你总是这么说。

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:40.720
<v Ira Glass>And then, still lying in bed. He explains the whole thing perfectly. It would be impossible to say this better.
<v Ira Glass>然后，仍然躺在床上。他完美地解释了整个事情。不可能说得更好。

00:55:40.720 --> 00:56:05.370
<v Morgan Peach>(SLEEPILY) It's almost as if the sleeping is that much sweeter when you have to get up or you think you have to get up, and then you don't.
<v Morgan Peach>（睡眼惺忪地）当必须起来或你认为你必须起来时，而你没有起来，睡觉就显得更甜了。

